<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Dance · Celebration · date unknown · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">La Bamba</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A lively folk song and dance from Veracruz in Mexico, sung at weddings. In 1958 Ritchie Valens made it a rock and roll hit.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>son jarocho musicians of Veracruz, Mexico</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Veracruz, Mexico</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional, from the time of the viceroys of Mexico; oldest known recording 1938 or 1939</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Dance · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional son jarocho from Veracruz; Ritchie Valens's 1958 arrangement is not in the public domain)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-bamba">La Bamba</a>&quot; is a Mexican folk song from the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and an example of son jarocho, a style of the region that combines Spanish (Andalusian), Indigenous, Afro-Mexican and Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> musical elements. It is typically played on one or two arpas jarochas (harps) with the jarana jarocha and the requinto jarocho, both string instruments. Its tune stays the same, but its words vary greatly, because performers often improvise verses as they play; versions by popular groups such as Mariachi Vargas de Tecalitlán and Los Pregoneros del Puerto have survived because of their fame.</p><p>The song is said to have begun as satire on the late and futile efforts of the viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to defend the people of the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>. His ships were old and leaky, and in the song a young sailor frantically works the pump, boasting that he, who keeps the ship afloat, is really its captain; the fast rhythm and hand movements of the dance comically imitate pumping water. The name also recalls a Spanish dance called bamba, popular at the time, which sounds close to bomba, a pump. The oldest known references to the song come from the town of Alvarado. Other explanations of the name, none certain, link it to the Spanish verb bambolear, &quot;to sway&quot;, or to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>: to the Bamba people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> or the Bambara of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><p>In a traditional version performed at weddings and in ballet folklórico shows, couples use intricate footwork to tie a bow that stands for their union. The custom of improvising verses comes from here: wedding guests were encouraged to make the song their own for the newlyweds, though verses taken as insults could start fights, and even the drawing of weapons.</p><p>The oldest known recording was made by Álvaro Hernández Ortiz, under the name El Jarocho, and released by Victor Records in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1938 or 1939. According to a 1945 article in Life, the American bandleader Everett Hoagland brought the song and its dance to Ciro's nightclub in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, where it became popular; the presidential candidate Miguel Alemán Valdés adopted it for his successful campaign, and later in 1945 Arthur Murray introduced the music and dance at the Stork Club in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City. Andrés <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> and his brother Victor recorded a popular version around 1945–46, and the song appeared in the Mexican film Rayando el Sol (1946) and the MGM musical Fiesta (1947). In the 1950s the folk singers William Clauson and Cynthia Gooding recorded it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>Ritchie <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> learned the song as a boy from his cousin Dickie Cota. Proud of his Mexican heritage, he at first hesitated to merge it with rock and roll, fearing to commercialise a folk song; he spoke little Spanish and learned the words by sound. His 1958 rock and roll version, issued by Del-Fi as the B-side of &quot;Donna&quot;, became a top 40 hit in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and it is the only song not in English on Rolling Stone's list of the 500 greatest songs of all time. In 2018 the Library of Congress chose it for the National Recording Registry. Los Lobos recorded the song for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-bamba">La Bamba</a>, the 1987 film of Valens's life, ending with a coda in the traditional style of Veracruz; their version topped the Billboard Hot 100.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A fast, playful dance song whose verses are made up afresh by the singers, often to honour or tease a newly married couple. Its oldest story mocks the powerful through a boastful sailor working the pump of a leaking ship.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="181439" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn sg-genius-btn-en" data-genius="10083755" data-what="English translation">Show the English translation from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics and their translation in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Ritchie-valens-la-bamba-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a> &middot; <a href="https://genius.com/Genius-english-translations-ritchie-valens-la-bamba-english-translation-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">its English translation</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
